    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Umayyads turned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> into a hereditary monarchy ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, from 661 to 750.</li><li>Their conquests reached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, spreading Arabic and laying the ground for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> across three continents.</li><li>Favouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> over non-Arab converts bred revolt; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> overthrew them in 750, though a survivor founded a new Umayyad state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Abd al-Malik's gold dinar of 697 dropped Byzantine and Persian imagery for Arabic Quranic inscriptions, creating a distinctly Islamic coinage.</p></aside>
    </section>

