    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muawiya-i.jpg" alt="Muawiya I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiya I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arab-Sasanian_coin_of_Muawiyah_I,_struck_at_the_Fasa_mint_in_Darabjird_(Fars).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LouisAragon (uploader), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Umayyad Caliphate was established in 661 CE after the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, the fourth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> and the cousin and son-in-law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan</a>, the long-serving governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the most powerful figure in the Islamic world, secured recognition as caliph and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> the seat of the caliphate in place of Ali's base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and the earlier capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. Muawiyah belonged to the Umayyad clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, the clan that had led Meccan opposition to Muhammad until the Prophet's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 630, and his accession transformed the caliphate from a leadership chosen by consultation into a hereditary monarchy rooted in Syria. His nomination of his son Yazid as successor led directly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a> (civil war, 680–692), during which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>, Ali's son and Muhammad's grandson, was killed with a small band of followers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> in 680 by forces sent by Yazid's governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubayd-allah-ibn-ziyad">Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad</a>. Karbala became the defining martyrdom of Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and deepened the division between what would become the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia traditions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwanids">Marwanid</a> branch of the dynasty, founded by Marwan I (r. 684–685), recovered the caliphate from the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zubayrids">Zubayrid</a> claimant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a> under Abd al-Malik (r. 685–705) and proved more stable and administratively innovative than Muawiyah's line; Abd al-Malik also built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, completed around 691. Under Abd al-Malik and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a> (r. 705–715), whose reign saw the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Great Mosque of Damascus</a>, the caliphate expanded simultaneously into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> in one of the most rapid territorial expansions in history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mediterranean.jpg" alt="Mediterranean" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">of the modification : Eric Gaba (Sting), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Umayyad economy rested on the agricultural surpluses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, among the most productive farming regions of the ancient world, supplemented by the revenues of a vast trade network linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s fiscal system was transformed under Abd al-Malik, who replaced the inherited practice of running separate Byzantine and Sasanian administrative and monetary systems in their former territories with a unified Islamic system. His coinage reform of the 690s, culminating in the aniconic gold dinar of 697, abandoned Byzantine and Sasanian imagery in favour of Quranic inscriptions in Arabic, standardising currency across the empire and asserting Islamic authority over the commercial system. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> poll tax on non-Muslim subjects (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">kharaj</a> land tax were the main sources of revenue, which created a structural tension: conversion reduced the population liable to jizya, and some governors, notably al-Hajjaj in Iraq, continued to tax converts or sent them back to the land. The pious caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-abd-al-aziz">Umar II</a> (r. 717–720) attempted to relieve converts of these burdens, but the problem persisted to the end of the dynasty. Arab tribal elites, above all those of Syria, were the primary beneficiaries of the Umayyad system: Arab warriors drew stipends registered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a>, while the conquered lands were taxed through existing administrative structures with little disruption to production. Mediterranean trade in grain, oil, textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> and luxury goods continued much as it had under Byzantine rule, with Arab merchants increasingly active alongside Greek, Coptic, Syrian Christian and Jewish commercial networks.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours.jpg" alt="Battle of Tours" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours">Battle of Tours</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Steuben_-_Bataille_de_Poitiers.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charles de Steuben, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The armies of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> were the most expansionist force of the seventh and early eighth centuries, achieving a pace and scale of conquest that rivalled the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and proved far more lasting. The decisive early victories belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> period that preceded the dynasty: at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-yarmuk">Yarmuk</a> in 636 Arab forces destroyed the Byzantine army defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-qadisiyyah">al-Qadisiyya</a> around 636–637 they broke the Sasanian army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, opening the heartlands of two ancient empires that had exhausted one another in decades of warfare. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiyah</a> himself had been a central figure in these conquests as governor of Syria, where he built the first Arab war fleet, raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, and defeated the Byzantine navy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-masts">Battle of the Masts</a> in 654 or 655. The Umayyads carried the conquests in both directions at once. In the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> around 670, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> was subdued, and in 711 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tariq-ibn-ziyad">Tariq ibn Ziyad</a> crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-conquest-of-hispania">Umayyad conquest of Hispania</a> and overthrowing the Visigothic kingdom; raids beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> were checked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours">Battle of Tours</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>) in 732. In the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-conquest-of-sind">Umayyad conquest of Sind</a> in 711–712. The failure of the First Arab siege of Constantinople in the 670s and of a second siege in 717–718 demonstrated the limits of Arab naval and siege capability against the city's formidable walls and the devastating effectiveness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire</a>, while defeats at the hands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in 730 and the Turgesh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> marked further limits of expansion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/damascus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/damascus.jpg" alt="Damascus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Damascus,_Syria,_Panoramic_view_of_Damascus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Umayyad government was characterised by an Arab tribal elite's attempt to administer an empire of extraordinary ethnic, religious and linguistic diversity, a challenge it met with pragmatic eclecticism rather than systematic redesign. The Byzantine administrative framework in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was largely preserved, with Greek-speaking officials continuing to manage taxation, records and local government until Abd al-Malik's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabisation">arabisation</a> reforms of the late 690s made Arabic the language of the central registers. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, Sasanian administrative traditions were likewise maintained through existing personnel, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dihqan">dihqans</a>, the Persian landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, continuing to collect taxes from village communities, until the Persian-language registers were also arabised. This pragmatic approach, governing through existing institutions while asserting Muslim political supremacy, reduced disruption but produced a state identified more with Arab rule than with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as a universal community. The privileging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> over non-Arab Muslims (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a>) in military stipends, appointments and social status was the dynasty's most politically consequential choice, generating deep resentment among Persian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and other converts that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a> would later mobilise. Provincial government was entrusted to powerful governors, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hajjaj-ibn-yusuf">al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf</a>, who ruled Iraq and the east from 694 to 714 with notorious severity. Islamic law was administered by appointed judges (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a>), though customary and pre-Islamic legal traditions continued to govern much of daily life among non-Arab and non-Muslim communities. The court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, together with the desert palaces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qusayr-amra">Qusayr Amra</a>, adopted elements of Byzantine and Persian royal display, which pious critics condemned as incompatible with the egalitarian ideals of early Islam.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock.jpg" alt="Dome of the Rock" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalem_DomeoftheRock_J65_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ludvig14, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Umayyad Caliphate's historical impact was among the most consequential of any empire, reshaping the religious, linguistic and cultural geography of three continents within less than a century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a> ended Byzantine rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, beginning a transformation of these lands into predominantly Arabic-speaking and ultimately Muslim societies, a change that proved irreversible across most of the conquered territory. Conversion, however, was gradual: in Egypt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, long centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, Muslims probably became a majority only over the following centuries, and substantial Christian and Jewish communities survived throughout the region. The incorporation of Persia brought Persian cultural and intellectual traditions into Islamic civilisation, a synthesis that would shape Islamic statecraft, philosophy and literature for centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-conquest-of-hispania">Arab conquest of Spain</a> in the 710s established Muslim rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> for nearly eight centuries, creating the civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, whose philosophical, scientific and literary achievements later helped transmit Greek and Arabic learning to medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation. The spread of Arabic as a language of government, commerce, religion and scholarship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> created an intellectual community of unprecedented geographic extent, making Arabic one of the great vehicles of pre-modern learning. Umayyad architecture, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Great Mosque of Damascus</a>, set enduring models for Islamic religious building. The establishment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, a consequence above all of the early Arab conquests that the Umayyads consolidated and extended, shaped the political, cultural and religious development of this vast region for the following thirteen centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala.jpg" alt="Battle of Karbala" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Battle of Karbala</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Battle_of_Karbala_-_Abbas_Al-Musavi_-_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Persian Painter Abbas Al-Musavi., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fall of the Umayyad Caliphate in 750 CE was the product of structural contradictions rooted in its founding political choices, which accumulated over nearly a century until they produced a revolutionary coalition too broad to suppress. The central contradiction was the dynasty's identification with Arab tribal supremacy in an empire whose Muslim community was becoming increasingly non-Arab: as the number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a> grew, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, the gap between the egalitarian message of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the hierarchical practice of Umayyad rule became intolerable. The Muslims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, both mawali and settled Arab colonists, formed the social base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a>, and the hostility of the Shia, who had never accepted Umayyad legitimacy after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a>, supplied further revolutionary energy. The great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-revolt">Berber Revolt</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, which broke out in 739–740 over discriminatory taxation, demands for slaves and unequal treatment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> Muslims who had fought in the conquests, showed that the periphery was prepared to contest Umayyad authority by force. The crisis deepened with the bitter rivalry between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> (northern Arab) and Yaman (Qahtan, or southern Arab) tribal factions, and after the murder of al-Walid II in 744 the dynasty descended into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-fitna">Third Fitna</a>, paralysing its armies precisely when unity was most needed. The Abbasid Revolution, proclaimed in Khorasan in 747 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a>, swept westward with extraordinary speed: in January 750 the last Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan II</a>, was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-great-zab">Battle of the Great Zab</a>, pursued into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> massacred most of the Umayyad family; the most famous survivor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a>, escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a> in 756. The new dynasty shifted the centre of the caliphate to Iraq, where al-Mansur founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 762, marking the end of Umayyad rule in the east and the beginning of the Abbasid era.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Umayyad Caliphate's supreme historical achievement was the creation of one of the largest empires the world had yet seen within a single century, an expansion so rapid and so enduring in its religious and linguistic consequences that it remains a foundational political event of the Islamic world.</em></p>

